1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,400
A hydrocarbon question can ask you to name

2
00:00:02,440 --> 00:00:05,600
a molecule, sort its isomers, or predict what

3
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:06,320
it makes.

4
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:09,480
Each one here comes down to three checks.

5
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:11,120
Which bonds does it have?

6
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:14,640
What's added or swapped, and under what conditions?

7
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:17,280
And do the atoms and the name check

8
00:00:17,360 --> 00:00:17,960
out?

9
00:00:18,320 --> 00:00:21,080
Here are four HSC-style questions.

10
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:23,520
Pause and try each one, then we'll work

11
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:25,440
it through, one step at a time.

12
00:00:27,467 --> 00:00:28,586
Question one.

13
00:00:28,907 --> 00:00:32,266
Two hydrocarbons, A and B, are shown as

14
00:00:32,307 --> 00:00:33,786
skeletal formulas.

15
00:00:34,107 --> 00:00:36,826
Give the IUPAC name of each compound.

16
00:00:37,147 --> 00:00:40,426
Then identify the type of structural isomerism shown

17
00:00:40,547 --> 00:00:43,466
by A and B, and justify your answer.

18
00:00:43,627 --> 00:00:44,826
Pause here to answer.

19
00:00:56,034 --> 00:00:57,073
Start with A.

20
00:00:57,474 --> 00:01:00,113
Its longest chain has six carbons and one

21
00:01:00,194 --> 00:01:02,353
double bond, so it's a hexene.

22
00:01:02,914 --> 00:01:05,233
Number from the end nearer the double bond,

23
00:01:05,274 --> 00:01:09,033
and it starts at carbon two: hex-two-ene.

24
00:01:09,474 --> 00:01:10,353
Now B.

25
00:01:10,594 --> 00:01:12,833
Its longest chain has five carbons, with a

26
00:01:12,954 --> 00:01:14,113
methyl branch.

27
00:01:14,194 --> 00:01:16,513
The double bond wins the numbering, so count

28
00:01:16,594 --> 00:01:18,033
from the right-hand end.

29
00:01:18,194 --> 00:01:20,393
That puts the double bond at carbon two,

30
00:01:20,434 --> 00:01:24,833
and the methyl on carbon four: four-methylpent-two-ene.

31
00:01:26,834 --> 00:01:28,353
Now count the atoms.

32
00:01:28,594 --> 00:01:32,193
Each is C six H twelve: six carbons,

33
00:01:32,274 --> 00:01:35,393
and one double bond, so two fewer hydrogens

34
00:01:35,474 --> 00:01:36,273
than the alkane.

35
00:01:36,754 --> 00:01:41,073
Same formula, different structures, so they're structural isomers.

36
00:01:41,314 --> 00:01:44,193
Both are alkenes, so it's not functional group

37
00:01:44,274 --> 00:01:45,233
isomerism.

38
00:01:45,474 --> 00:01:48,433
What changed is the carbon skeleton: A is

39
00:01:48,474 --> 00:01:51,153
a straight chain, and B is branched.

40
00:01:51,234 --> 00:01:52,913
So they're chain isomers.

41
00:01:54,934 --> 00:01:57,413
The classic mistake is numbering B from the

42
00:01:57,494 --> 00:01:59,253
left, the way you read it.

43
00:01:59,494 --> 00:02:02,253
That gives two-methylpent-three-ene.

44
00:02:02,534 --> 00:02:04,453
The branch gets the low number, but the

45
00:02:04,534 --> 00:02:06,773
double bond is named in the ending, so

46
00:02:06,934 --> 00:02:09,173
it gets first claim on the low numbers.

47
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:12,000
Question two.

48
00:02:12,080 --> 00:02:16,400
Pent-two-ene reacts with hydrogen chloride at room temperature.

49
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:20,240
In a separate reaction, pentane reacts with chlorine

50
00:02:20,320 --> 00:02:21,520
under UV light.

51
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:25,520
For each reaction, identify the type of reaction,

52
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:29,040
and write a balanced equation, using structural formulas,

53
00:02:29,080 --> 00:02:31,920
for the formation of one organic product.

54
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:33,680
Pause here to answer.

55
00:02:44,900 --> 00:02:46,660
Start with pent-two-ene.

56
00:02:47,060 --> 00:02:49,620
It has a double bond, and hydrogen chloride

57
00:02:49,780 --> 00:02:52,500
needs no light or catalyst, so this is

58
00:02:52,580 --> 00:02:55,140
addition: the H goes on one carbon of

59
00:02:55,180 --> 00:02:57,060
the double bond, and the Cl on the

60
00:02:57,140 --> 00:02:57,460
other.

61
00:02:57,940 --> 00:03:00,740
Pent-two-ene isn't the same at both ends, so

62
00:03:00,780 --> 00:03:02,660
there are two possible products.

63
00:03:02,900 --> 00:03:04,980
Put the Cl on carbon two, and you

64
00:03:05,060 --> 00:03:06,820
get two-chloropentane.

65
00:03:07,060 --> 00:03:09,380
Three-chloropentane is just as correct.

66
00:03:09,780 --> 00:03:12,820
Either way, two molecules make one, and nothing

67
00:03:12,860 --> 00:03:13,540
is lost.

68
00:03:15,567 --> 00:03:16,686
Now pentane.

69
00:03:17,007 --> 00:03:19,486
It has only single bonds, so it can't

70
00:03:19,567 --> 00:03:19,966
add.

71
00:03:20,447 --> 00:03:23,566
The UV light gives the energy for substitution:

72
00:03:23,647 --> 00:03:26,126
one hydrogen swaps for a chlorine, and that

73
00:03:26,207 --> 00:03:28,206
hydrogen leaves as H Cl.

74
00:03:28,687 --> 00:03:30,686
Swap one on an end carbon, and you

75
00:03:30,807 --> 00:03:32,766
get one-chloropentane.

76
00:03:33,087 --> 00:03:36,526
Notice that both reactions make a chloropentane, C

77
00:03:36,607 --> 00:03:38,446
five H eleven Cl.

78
00:03:38,927 --> 00:03:41,406
What tells them apart is the starting bonds,

79
00:03:41,487 --> 00:03:43,006
and the H Cl.

80
00:03:45,034 --> 00:03:47,753
The classic mistake is treating pentane like an

81
00:03:47,914 --> 00:03:50,713
alkene, and putting a chlorine on two neighbouring

82
00:03:50,794 --> 00:03:51,633
carbons.

83
00:03:51,914 --> 00:03:55,113
Count the atoms: that product is two hydrogens

84
00:03:55,194 --> 00:03:57,593
short, so the equation can't balance.

85
00:03:58,074 --> 00:04:00,713
An alkane has no double bond to open.

86
00:04:02,734 --> 00:04:03,893
Question three.

87
00:04:04,174 --> 00:04:08,013
A hydrocarbon, X, reacts with excess chlorine at

88
00:04:08,094 --> 00:04:10,133
room temperature, in the dark.

89
00:04:10,414 --> 00:04:13,573
The only organic product is one, one, two,

90
00:04:13,614 --> 00:04:15,733
two-tetrachlorobutane.

91
00:04:16,014 --> 00:04:19,373
Identify X, and explain your reasoning, including the

92
00:04:19,454 --> 00:04:20,693
type of reaction.

93
00:04:20,974 --> 00:04:22,413
Pause here to answer.

94
00:04:33,634 --> 00:04:35,673
Work backwards from the product.

95
00:04:35,954 --> 00:04:38,353
It's in the dark, with no UV light,

96
00:04:38,434 --> 00:04:40,193
so this isn't substitution.

97
00:04:40,674 --> 00:04:41,873
Chlorine has added.

98
00:04:42,434 --> 00:04:45,313
The product has four chlorine atoms, so two

99
00:04:45,394 --> 00:04:48,193
chlorine molecules added to each X molecule.

100
00:04:48,834 --> 00:04:51,553
A double bond can open only once, so

101
00:04:51,634 --> 00:04:53,553
it would add one chlorine molecule.

102
00:04:54,114 --> 00:04:57,313
Adding twice needs two extra bonds: a triple

103
00:04:57,394 --> 00:04:59,633
bond, or two double bonds.

104
00:05:01,634 --> 00:05:02,353
Now where.

105
00:05:02,434 --> 00:05:05,393
All four chlorines are on carbons one and

106
00:05:05,474 --> 00:05:06,913
two, two on each.

107
00:05:07,394 --> 00:05:09,873
So both additions happened across the same pair

108
00:05:09,914 --> 00:05:10,753
of carbons.

109
00:05:11,234 --> 00:05:13,713
That needs a triple bond, between carbon one

110
00:05:13,874 --> 00:05:14,833
and carbon two.

111
00:05:15,474 --> 00:05:17,393
Take the chlorines off, and put the triple

112
00:05:17,474 --> 00:05:18,433
bond back.

113
00:05:18,754 --> 00:05:22,913
X is but-one-yne, C H triple bond C,

114
00:05:22,994 --> 00:05:25,313
C H two, C H three.

115
00:05:25,394 --> 00:05:28,673
Check: C four H six, plus two Cl

116
00:05:28,834 --> 00:05:31,873
two, gives C four H six Cl four.

117
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:36,780
The classic mistake is but-one-ene.

118
00:05:36,900 --> 00:05:39,100
Its double bond is in the right place,

119
00:05:39,140 --> 00:05:41,660
but it can add only one chlorine molecule,

120
00:05:41,700 --> 00:05:46,380
giving one, two-dichlorobutane, with just two chlorines.

121
00:05:48,400 --> 00:05:49,360
Question four.

122
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:53,280
X is an open-chain hydrocarbon with one multiple

123
00:05:53,360 --> 00:05:53,760
bond.

124
00:05:54,240 --> 00:05:57,520
Its molecular formula is C five H eight,

125
00:05:57,560 --> 00:06:00,800
and it decolourises bromine water at room temperature,

126
00:06:01,040 --> 00:06:02,080
without UV light.

127
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:05,520
A student concludes that X is an alkene,

128
00:06:05,640 --> 00:06:07,680
because it decolourises bromine water.

129
00:06:08,160 --> 00:06:10,000
Evaluate the student's conclusion.

130
00:06:10,640 --> 00:06:11,920
Pause here to answer.

131
00:06:23,134 --> 00:06:25,373
Start with what the test shows.

132
00:06:25,694 --> 00:06:28,733
Losing the orange colour means bromine has added,

133
00:06:28,814 --> 00:06:30,733
so X has a double bond or a

134
00:06:30,814 --> 00:06:31,533
triple bond.

135
00:06:32,014 --> 00:06:33,693
That rules out an alkane.

136
00:06:34,014 --> 00:06:38,093
But alkenes and alkynes both decolourise bromine water,

137
00:06:38,174 --> 00:06:40,613
so the test alone can't tell you which.

138
00:06:40,894 --> 00:06:42,973
The student has read more into the result

139
00:06:43,054 --> 00:06:44,013
than it can show.

140
00:06:46,034 --> 00:06:47,313
So use the formula.

141
00:06:47,634 --> 00:06:50,353
With five carbons, an alkene would be C

142
00:06:50,434 --> 00:06:53,553
n H two n, C five H ten.

143
00:06:54,034 --> 00:06:56,593
An alkyne is C n H two n

144
00:06:56,674 --> 00:07:00,033
minus two, C five H eight, which matches

145
00:07:00,194 --> 00:07:00,593
X.

146
00:07:01,074 --> 00:07:03,593
So the student's conclusion is incorrect.

147
00:07:03,794 --> 00:07:06,753
The test shows that X is unsaturated, and

148
00:07:06,834 --> 00:07:09,153
the formula shows it's an alkyne, such as

149
00:07:09,314 --> 00:07:10,593
pent-one-yne.

150
00:07:10,634 --> 00:07:13,233
It would add two bromine molecules, not one.

151
00:07:15,234 --> 00:07:17,953
The classic mistake is treating bromine water as

152
00:07:17,994 --> 00:07:20,433
a test for double bonds only.

153
00:07:20,474 --> 00:07:23,473
It's a test for unsaturation: a double or

154
00:07:23,554 --> 00:07:24,513
a triple bond.

155
00:07:26,534 --> 00:07:29,413
So here's how to start any hydrocarbon question.

156
00:07:29,894 --> 00:07:33,413
First, find the bonds: trace the longest chain

157
00:07:33,494 --> 00:07:35,573
with the multiple bond, and number from its

158
00:07:35,734 --> 00:07:36,733
nearer end.

159
00:07:37,014 --> 00:07:39,573
An alkane with a halogen and UV light

160
00:07:39,734 --> 00:07:43,413
means substitution: swap one hydrogen, and add H

161
00:07:43,494 --> 00:07:43,893
X.

162
00:07:44,374 --> 00:07:47,253
A double or triple bond means addition, one

163
00:07:47,334 --> 00:07:49,493
piece on each carbon, and a triple bond

164
00:07:49,574 --> 00:07:50,453
adds twice.

165
00:07:50,934 --> 00:07:53,253
Then count the atoms, and check the formula

166
00:07:53,414 --> 00:07:54,613
fits the family.

167
00:07:54,934 --> 00:07:57,733
Next, alcohols, and what an O H group

168
00:07:57,814 --> 00:07:59,013
does to a molecule.

169
00:08:01,034 --> 00:08:02,473
Here are the key notes.

170
00:08:02,714 --> 00:08:04,633
Pause the video if you need more time

171
00:08:04,714 --> 00:08:05,513
to copy them.
